WEBVTT


00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.330
Babies and kids heads
are larger than we think.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.670
In relation to their neck.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:06.900
They're pretty big.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:14.010
A newborn head circumference can be
about 15 inches and an adults is 20.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.140
But a newborn neck opening is
usually only about nine inches or so.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:25.440
So to make a pullover neck opening large
enough, we leave one of the shoulders open

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.420
and secure it with buttons.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.650
Make it easier to pull that
pull over over the child's head.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.240
Let's think about how to do
this with any knitting pattern.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.300
Since we're leaving one shoulder open, we
need a bit of an overlap to add buttons

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.260
and button holes.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.240
When the sweater's buttoned, the
original seam line will match.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.040
So we're going to add a
few extra rows to the back.

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:57.735
And finish the corresponding
front with buttonholes.

00:00:59.905 --> 00:01:05.144
Now if you look closely, I got my lefts
and rights mixed up and I put my opening

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:07.305
on the right side of my sweater.

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:09.390
No big deal.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.429
This is a kid sweater, but I believe it's
traditional for this style to have the

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:17.880
button holes on the left
as the sweater is worn.

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:19.800
Here's my back.

00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:25.380
When it came time to scrap off, I just
knit a few rows of garter stitch for the

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.100
extension.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.710
Now I followed the pattern all the way up
to that shoulder bind off area and then

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.990
finished that back and just added
a few extra rows for the extension.

00:01:38.475 --> 00:01:42.855
I chose to use garter stitch
because stockinette rolls.

00:01:43.845 --> 00:01:48.795
If you're a stitch pattern doesn't roll,
you could just continue using your stitch

00:01:48.795 --> 00:01:50.235
pattern for the extension.

00:01:51.435 --> 00:01:55.125
You could also knit or hand
manipulate some ribbing.

00:01:57.215 --> 00:01:58.895
Now to prevent bulk.

00:01:58.895 --> 00:02:02.465
I really prefer this extension
just to be one layer of knitting.

00:02:02.465 --> 00:02:07.005
So a hem doesn't really work well,
especially with heavier yarns.

00:02:08.485 --> 00:02:12.445
To create garter stitch over
these small shoulder sections.

00:02:12.805 --> 00:02:13.795
Use a garter bar.

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:20.775
You could reform the stitches or use a
hand knitting needle to remove, turn and

00:02:20.775 --> 00:02:26.235
rehang the work repeatedly to create
those purl bumps of the garter stitch.

00:02:27.735 --> 00:02:33.525
Now one thing to remember, the gauge
of garter stitch is much wider than

00:02:33.525 --> 00:02:34.525
stockinette.

00:02:35.265 --> 00:02:40.304
So I reduced a few stitches before I
even started creating those garter rows.

00:02:41.475 --> 00:02:47.565
In other words, my 16 stitch shoulder
seam was reduced to 13 stitches for the

00:02:47.565 --> 00:02:48.315
extension.

00:02:52.364 --> 00:02:58.845
When I was knitting the front, I scrapped
off that shoulder four rows or about an

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:02.295
inch before my pattern
instructed me to bind off.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.930
For my pattern, my neck line shaping
was complete at this point, and on that

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.310
shoulder that was going to be joined,
I just scrapped off four rows early.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.110
When the front piece was completed, that
neckline was shaped and the other shoulder

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:20.800
was done.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:27.280
I picked up those stitches with a hand
knitting needle, reduced it down to 13 and

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.500
hand knit a few rows in garter stitch.

00:03:30.225 --> 00:03:33.825
I worked three button holes
in the center of this section.

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:39.435
In other words, I did not extend
the length of my front shoulder.

00:03:40.275 --> 00:03:44.865
I worked my shoulder the same number
of rows that my pattern told me to.

00:03:45.885 --> 00:03:47.625
I hand knit my garter stitch.

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:49.215
You could hand manipulate.

00:03:49.425 --> 00:03:50.715
I just found it easier.

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:54.450
Now that everything's done.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:04:00.630
When I overlap the buttonhole section,
over that extension, I've maintained my

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.760
original seam line, the original
neckline open dimension remains the same.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:11.280
I'll maintain that overlap and carefully
hang both sections when I pick up for my

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.000
sleeve.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:18.899
Now I just need to find a couple of cute
little pink buttons and I'll be all set.

